uestion/id #uuid "c35be6ee-fc39-5871-93ce-fbaaec647adb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106763" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-106763/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106763"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
